🧭 My Journey (so far)

I started from zero CUDA knowledge — just a student with a GTX 1660 Ti, a bunch of curiosity, and a desire to build something real.

Over the past weeks, I:

· Fought through CUDA install hell (11.8, VS2019, v142, driver versions, you name it).
· Learned CUDA C++ from scratch – __global__, __device__, __shared__, cudaMalloc, cudaMemcpy, thread indexing, block/grid configuration, and even some shared memory optimization.
· Wrote my own LeNet in CUDA – forward pass only, but it worked.
· Moved to PyTorch for faster iteration – built a full training pipeline for a 6‑class emergency‑response image classifier (Heimlich maneuver scenarios: infant, child, teen, adult, pregnant, obese).
· Collected and augmented a small dataset (648 train / 432 val) using custom download scripts and offline augmentation.
· Explored nearly every regularization trick: Dropout, weight decay, label smoothing, EMA, gradient clipping, mixed precision (AMP), and GPU‑side augmentation with Kornia.
· Trained multiple ResNet‑18 variants – tried cosine annealing with warm restarts, ReduceLROnPlateau, and even CutMix (though the best stable run hit ~78% validation accuracy).
· Added practical features – keyboard‑controlled pause/resume (Ctrl+U), GPU/CPU temperature monitoring, and early stopping.

---

🎯 My Goal

I want to build a lightweight, deployable AI model that can recognize which category a person belongs to, and suggest the correct Heimlich maneuver procedure (abdominal thrust vs chest thrust vs back blows).

It’s not about 99% accuracy — it’s about understanding the full stack: from data collection → model training → deployment.
Eventually, I want to run inference without PyTorch (ONNX or LibTorch) on a clean machine, and maybe even embed it into a simple demo app.

Still, I am a beginner.I have to learn CUDA first. But the journey of 1000 miles begins with a single step, so that what I'm doing!

